    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mount Laguna rises to about 6,000 feet, a pine‑and‑oak forest perched on the eastern edge of Cleveland National Forest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Laguna%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village hosts a general store, rustic lodge, cabins, a local restaurant, a rural post office, and campgrounds beside the Pacific Crest Trail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Laguna%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Seasonally, the Kumeyaay (Diegueño) people hunted and gathered here, with archaeological evidence showing use back 9,000 years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        6,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Mount_Laguna,_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mount Laguna lies within the Laguna Mountains, a rugged subrange of the Peninsular Ranges, featuring pine, oak, chaparral, and open meadows.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Mount_Laguna,_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During the Cold War, the Mount Laguna Air Force Station operated on the peak before closing in the late 20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Laguna%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The climate is warm‑summer Mediterranean, with dry summers, wet winters, and occasional summer monsoons arriving from the Gulf of California.",
